# Why does insurance make people less careful?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is moral hazard? Why do deductibles and co-payments exist?"
next: {"title":"Can markets fix their own information problems?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/51-can-markets-fix-their-own-information.cast"}

## Free care, more care
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Free care
      - Pay 95%
    values:
      - 2170
      - 1325
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: each
    colors:
      - green
      - red
    icons:
      - stethoscope
      - money with wings
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 2500
    y_label: $ per person per year (2011 $)
    x_label: RAND experiment, 1974–81
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 150
      w: 520
      h: 500
sources:
    - id: aef2013
      title: The RAND Health Insurance Experiment, Three Decades Later
      authors: Aron-Dine, A., Einav, L. & Finkelstein, A.
      year: 2013
      finding: '1974–1981: over 5,800 people in about 2,000 households at six US sites, assigned to plans with 0, 25, 50 or 95% coinsurance (plus a mixed and an individual-deductible plan) for 3–5 years, out-of-pocket capped at 5, 10 or 15% of income up to $1,000. Average annual spending per person: free care $2,170 (2011 $); the 95% plan $845 (39%) lower (Table 2).'
      doi: 10.1257/jep.27.1.197

    text facts "1974–81\n~2,000 households\nplans at random" cites ["aef2013"] x 800 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text drop "−39%" #b5482e cites ["aef2013"] x 800 y 500 font_size 64 hidden true
    text mh "Moral hazard" x 800 y 600 font_size 44 hidden true
    box k1 "Before: less careful" x 818 y 370 width 340 height 100 icon lock icon_side left font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Free care, more care"

Does insurance make people less careful? In 1974, the United States set out to measure it, with a real experiment.
    draw axes

B: The RAND Health Insurance Experiment. About two thou (@draw facts@)sand households in six American towns and cities were assigned health insurance plans  (@highlight facts effect underline@)at random, for three to five years.

Some got all their care free. Others paid ninety-five percent of every bill themselves, up to a yearly cap tied to their income.
    highlight facts effect underline

    erase facts

B: The free-care families  (@draw bar_1@)spent about two thousand one hundred and seventy dollars a person a year, in 2011 dollars.

My hunch: nobody gets sick for fun, so the families who paid almost everything spent about the same.
    point at.ref bar_1 gesture tap

    ask "Drag the 'Pay 95%' bar to what those families spent per person." intro "What's your guess?" on bar_2 store g default "600" voice b
        right: About $1,325: you guessed {g} dollars.
        wrong: You guessed {g} dollars: here, about $1,325.

B: They didn't. They spent about thirteen hundred and twenty-five dolla (@draw drop@)rs, thirty-nine percent less.

Same kinds of families, assigned at random. The only difference was the price they paid at the doctor's door.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    pause 0.3

B: So free care didn't make people sick more often. It made each visit cheaper to the person deciding whether to go.
    highlight bar_2

B: That's mor (@draw mh@)al hazard: being insured changes what you do, because someone else pays part of the bill.

It comes in two kinds. Before a (@draw k1@) loss: you take less care, say you lock an insured bike less carefully.

After a loss: you use more of what the insurance pays for.
    box k2 "After: use more" x 818 y 240 width 340 height 100 icon stethoscope icon_side left font_size 28

B: RAND measured the second kind, and in health care it is the one that shows up in the bills.
    highlight k2 effect box

B: So is that extra care waste, or is it what insurance is for?
    erase k1 k2

    pause 0.5

## What is a free visit worth?
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 11
    'y':
      - 0
      - 210
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 150
      w: 640
      h: 450
vars:
    p:
      value: 100
      min: 0
      max: 100
    Qp:
      expr: (200-p)/20
    waste:
      expr: (100-p)^2/40
sources:
    - id: pauly1968
      title: 'The Economics of Moral Hazard: Comment'
      authors: Mark V. Pauly
      year: 1968
      finding: 'American Economic Review 58(3): 531–537. Insured patients'' extra use of care is ''a result not of moral perfidy, but of rational economic behavior''; with that response, full coverage need not be optimal.'

    axes ax x_label "Visits a year" y_label "$ per visit" cross corner ticks.x false ticks.y [0,100,200] hidden true
    curve worth thick #2f6b8f expr 200-20*x x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    text l_worth "Worth to you" #2f6b8f at.data [3.6,170] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve cost thick #3d3833 expr "100" x_from 0 x_to 10.8 hidden true
    text l_cost "Cost: $100" at.data [1.6,112] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot qstar at.on worth at.x 5 guides.x "Q* = 5" hidden true
    curve pay dashed thick #87a878 expr p x_from 0 x_to 10.8 hidden true
    text l_pay "You pay: ${p:0}" #5f8a52 x 840 y 600 font_size 28 hidden true
    dot qp #2f6b8f at.on worth at.x 5 bind {"at.x":"Qp"} hidden true
    label l_qp "{Qp:0} visits" below-left #2f6b8f attach_to qp hidden true
    region waste_area x_from.ref qstar x_to.ref qp between ["cost","worth"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.5 hidden true
    text readout "Waste: ${waste:0}" #b5482e x 840 y 530 font_size 34 hidden true
    scale guess x 690 y 470 width 265 label "Free care: visits a year?" min 0 max 10 value 10 ticks 10 hidden true
    text pauly "“not moral perfidy,\nbut rational\neconomic behavior”\n— Mark Pauly, 1968" cites ["pauly1968"] x 845 y 360 font_size 24 hidden true
    text deduct "Deductible:\nyou pay the\nfirst bills" #8a5fa8 x 845 y 360 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch mathcard work [{"tex":"\\text{Waste} = \\tfrac12 (100-p)(Q-5)"},{"tex":"p=0:\\ \\tfrac12 \\cdot 100 \\cdot 5 = 250"},{"tex":"p=40:\\ \\tfrac12 \\cdot 60 \\cdot 3 = 90"}] x 560 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "What is a free visit worth?"

B: If the extra care was waste, it should show on a diagram. Take Harbourtown's clinic, with illustrative numbers.
    draw ax

B: This line is what each visit in a year is worth to a typical resident. The first one, for a bad fever, is worth a lot. The tenth, for a sniffle, close to nothing.
    draw worth l_worth

And each visit costs the clinic a hundred dollars, whoever ends up paying.
    draw cost l_cost

B: Pay the full hundred yourself, and you go  (@draw qstar@)five times: every visit that's worth more to you than it costs.

Now make it free. How many visits a year?
    draw guess

    ask "Click on the line: how many visits a year with free care?" on guess store v default "6"
        right: Ten: you said {v}.
        wrong: You said {v}. Follow the blue line down to zero: here, ten.

    erase guess

    draw pay l_pay qp l_qp

B: Ten. At a price of zero you go whenever a visit is worth anything at all.
    animate p 0 duration 3

So visits six to ten each cost a hundred dollars and are worth less than that to the patient.
    point at.data [7.5,50] gesture circle

B: That triangle is t (@draw waste_area@)he waste: care that costs more than it's worth to the person getting it.

B: Here, two hundred and fifty dollars a person a year.
    draw readout

Mark Pauly put it bluntly in (@draw pauly@) 1968: this isn't moral perfidy. It's rational economic behaviour.

    erase pauly

    keep waste_area

B: Which is why insurers charge a co-payment, a fee per visit. Say forty dollars. Now the resident goes eight times, and the waste falls to ninety.
    animate p 40 duration 3

Then why not charge the full hundred? No waste at all.
    highlight cost l_cost

B: Because then you aren't insured. Every dollar you pay is risk handed back to you.
    highlight l_pay

B: A deductible splits (@draw deduct@) it: you pay the first, small bills yourself, the insurer pays the big ones.

    erase deduct waste_area_ghost

So every insurance contract trades the two: more protection, more waste. Try it: drag what you pay per visit and watch the triangle.
    highlight waste_area l_pay

    explore params ["p"]

    animate p 40 duration 1

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq

    fade ax worth l_worth cost l_cost qstar pay l_pay qp l_qp waste_area readout to 0.12

The triangle's height is the cost minus what you pay; its width, the extra visits.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

Free care: half of a hundred times five, two hundred and fifty.
    draw mathcard_line_2

A forty-dollar co-pay: half of sixty times three, ninety.
    draw mathcard_line_3

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

    fade ax worth l_worth cost l_cost qstar pay l_pay qp l_qp waste_area readout to 1

## Which care did they cut?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: An insurer would hire you tomorrow.
    good: Most of the fine print, read and understood.
    poor: Read the policy again, slowly.
    none: Good thing you're insured.
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 11
    'y':
      - 0
      - 210
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 150
      w: 640
      h: 450
vars: {"p":{"value":40,"fixed":true}}
sources:
    - id: lohr1986
      title: 'Use of Medical Care in the RAND Health Insurance Experiment: Diagnosis- and Service-Specific Analyses in a Randomized Controlled Trial'
      authors: Lohr, K. N., Brook, R. H., Kamberg, C. J. et al.
      year: 1986
      finding: Cost sharing cut the use of highly effective and of rarely effective care in about equal proportions; it was not selective. Medical Care 24(9) supplement; RAND R-3469.
      url: https://www.rand.org/pubs/reports/R3469.html
    - id: brook1983
      title: Does free care improve adults' health? Results from a randomized controlled trial
      authors: Brook, R. H., Ware, J. E., Rogers, W. H. et al.
      year: 1983
      finding: 'Little measurable effect on health for the average participant; free care improved blood-pressure control (and vision) for low-income participants with high blood pressure. N Engl J Med 309: 1426–1434.'
    - id: nyman1999
      title: The economics of moral hazard revisited
      authors: John A. Nyman
      year: 1999
      finding: 'Much of the extra care used by the insured is an income transfer from the healthy to the sick that lets them buy care they could not otherwise afford, and is welfare-increasing rather than waste. Journal of Health Economics 18(6): 811–824.'
    - id: aef2013
      title: The RAND Health Insurance Experiment, Three Decades Later
      authors: Aron-Dine, A., Einav, L. & Finkelstein, A.
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Spending responds to cost sharing: the 95% plan spent 39% less than free care; the response is robust to the experiment''s attrition and reporting problems.'
      doi: 10.1257/jep.27.1.197

    axes ax x_label "Visits a year" y_label "$ per visit" cross corner ticks.x false ticks.y [0,100,200] hidden true
    curve worth thick #2f6b8f expr 200-20*x x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    text l_worth "Worth to you" #2f6b8f at.data [3.6,170] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve cost thick #3d3833 expr "100" x_from 0 x_to 10.8 hidden true
    text l_cost "Cost: $100" at.data [1.6,112] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot qstar at.on worth at.x 5 guides.x "Q* = 5" hidden true
    curve pay dashed thick #87a878 expr "40" x_from 0 x_to 10.8 hidden true
    text l_pay "You pay: $40" #5f8a52 x 840 y 600 font_size 28 hidden true
    dot qp #2f6b8f at.on worth at.x 8 hidden true
    region ghost_tri x_from 5 x_to 10 between ["cost","worth"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.22 hidden true
    text l_ghost "Free-care waste?" #b5482e at.data [9.4,113] font_size 24 hidden true
    text cuts "Cuts:\nuseful ≈ useless" cites ["lohr1986"] x 840 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    curve health dashed #2f6b8f expr 260-20*x x_from 5 x_to 10 cites ["brook1983"] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    text l_health "Worth to health" #2f6b8f at.data [7.4,150] font_size 26 hidden true
    region notwaste x_from 5 x_to 8 between ["cost","worth"] style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    text l_notwaste "↑ not waste" #5f8a52 at.data [6.6,20] font_size 32 hidden true
    text conf1 "Cheaper care,\nmore care:\nconsensus" cites ["aef2013"] x 840 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    text conf2 "How much\nis waste:\ncontested" #b5482e cites ["nyman1999","brook1983"] x 840 y 300 font_size 36 hidden true
    text soft "Free check-ups,\ncheap chronic meds" #8a5fa8 at.data [5.5,196] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Which care did they cut?"

The diagram says the extra care is waste.
    draw ax worth l_worth cost l_cost qstar pay l_pay qp

Co- (@draw ghost_tri l_ghost@)pay, deductible, done.

B: But the line assumes the patient knows what a visit is worth. With a strange pain, do you?
    highlight l_worth

RAND checked. When people paid more, they cu (@draw cuts@)t care that doctors rated highly effective about as much as care that rarely helps.

B: And among the poorest participants with high blood pressure, free care b (@draw health l_health@)rought it down. Some of those skipped visits were worth far more than a hundred dollars.

So for them the true value line sits above the one w (@draw notwaste l_notwaste@)e drew, and part of the triangle isn't waste at all.

    erase cuts

B: The economist John Nyman goes further: often the extra care is just insurance doing its job, paying for treatment a sick person could not otherwise afford.
    highlight l_notwaste effect circle

So how sure are we? That people use more ca (@draw conf1@)re when it's cheaper: consensus, from RAND and the studies since.

B: How much o (@draw conf2@)f that extra care is waste: contested. It depends on who is cutting back, and on what.

Which is why the clumsy tools of deductibles and co-payments are often softene (@draw soft@)d: free check-ups, low fees for medicines that keep chronic illness in check.

    erase soft

B: So does insurance make people less careful? In health care, mostly it makes them use more, because the bill has moved to someone else.
    highlight qp effect circle

    pause 0.3

    quiz "Renters drive rental cars harder than their own cars. What is that?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: Moral hazard: the rental doesn't change who rents, it changes how they drive once someone else owns the car.
        wrong: Does the rental change who rents, or how they drive once they have the car? Here, moral hazard.
        + Moral hazard
        * Adverse selection
        * Neither

    quiz "A car insurer wants fewer claims for small scratches but full cover for a wreck. Which design?"
        right: A deductible: drivers pay the small scratches themselves, and the wreck is still fully covered.
        wrong: Which one leaves the big loss fully covered? Here, the $500 deductible.
        + A $500 deductible
        * Paying half of every claim
        * A higher premium for everyone

    quiz "Harbourtown's insurer pays the full value of any boat lost at sea. Which of these is moral hazard?" voice b
        right: Skipping the moorings: that's the careless kind, a change in what people do once they're insured. Old boats buying cover is adverse selection.
        wrong: Which one is about what people do after they are insured? Here, skipping the moorings.
        * Fishers with the oldest boats buy the most cover
        + Insured fishers skip checking their moorings before a storm
        * The insurer raises premiums after a bad year

And deductibles and co-payments are the price of that, paid in risk: a little exposure kept on you so the insurance can afford to cover the rest.
    highlight l_pay

Whether markets can deal with hidden behaviour on their own is the next question.
    highlight conf2 effect box

    pause 0.5

## Where next
